  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry today, though cooler with few clouds\nfollowing a backdoor cold front.\n\nA backdoor cold front is expected to make its way across southern \nNew England early this morning, clearing skies and ushering in \ndrier air. This front should be south of the coast by around 8 \nAM. Light N to NE winds develop behind this front, which will \nresult in lower temperatures across eastern MA. Farther inland, \ntemperatures should be similar or slightly higher than yesterday\ndue to more sunshine. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled tonight through Thursday, with several\nopportunities for showers and thunderstorms. Timing which days \nmay have better storm chances is still lower confidence. \n\nHigh pressure should become parked over the Maritimes, which\nwill provide resistance to pushing the lingering front out of\nour region until late this week. AS long as this front is\nnearby, there should be at least a risk for some showers, and\nperhaps a few thunderstorms. That said, not expecting it will be\nraining all week.\n\nRight now the two periods that appear to have the best chance \nof showers and storms looks to be tonight into the first part \nof Tuesday, and again later Wednesday afternoon into part of \nThursday. Tried to provide some timing in the forecast based on\nthe latest CAMs. Uncertainty in the precise timing and location\ndetails is still rather low. So, focused more on broader trends\nrather than trying to pin it down to the hour. Currently\nthinking the driest portion of this week should be late Tuesday\nnight into Wednesday morning.\n\nThe potential for thunderstorms to become strong to severe looks \nlimited, but a slightly better chance exists later Wednesday \ninto Thursday as a stronger shortwave aloft allows for at least\nsome of the more unstable warm sector to work its way in. \n  \nKEY MESSAGE 3...Mix of clouds and sun with diurnally driven spot\nshowers Friday into the early part of Labor Day weekend, but \ndrier weather most of the time. Dry and seasonable Sunday and on\nthe Monday holiday, with onshore breezes. \n\nStalled frontal boundary finally moves offshore Thursday, although \ncyclonic flow aloft will keep our weather pattern somewhat unsettled \nwith a mix of clouds and sun. Spot showers are possible Friday \nand Saturday afternoon but should be drier much of the time. \nBroad high pressure then works in from the north Sunday into \nLabor Day, leading to an onshore flow but with sunny and dry \nconditions.",
